    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d528">

                <pb facs="0657" n="555" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg555"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/2601</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the British Chargé</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lindsay</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-01-05"><hi rendition="#italic">January 5,
                        1920</hi>.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note No. 917 of December 31, 1919, regarding a proposed loan to the
                    Chinese Government.</p>
                <p>The Department is gratified to learn that the Japanese Government has withdrawn
                    its objection to the loan. In this connection I take pleasure in informing you
                    that I am just in receipt of a telegram from the American Ambassador at Peking
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">Paris</hi>] stating that the French Government has
                    withdrawn its contention that the disbandment of the troops should be a <hi
                        rendition="#italic">sine qua non</hi> to the issuance of the loan.</p>
                <p>There seems now to be no reason why the proposed advance to China should not be
                    made, and the Department is today informing the American Group to this
                    effect.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Breckinridge Long</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
